	"notes": "Notion's homepage is another stack of full-width marketing bands, and it uses the exact same trick as Figma's in this batch: the header and the row of customer logos reach all the way to both edges of the browser, but everything in between — the headline, the feature cards — sits inside a column capped at about 1252px and centred, so extra width on a big monitor just becomes empty space on the sides. The feature cards are arranged as a small 'bento' grid: two cards side by side, then one wide card spanning the row underneath them, all stacking to a single column on a phone. The one place this page does something different from its neighbours is the footer — most sites in this batch drop straight to one stacked column of links on a phone, but Notion keeps two columns side by side even at the narrowest width tested.",
